00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:32.780
On the Measure of Intelligence,
I mean, it's pretty amazing.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:36.380
Really encourage you check
that out after this session.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.780
But basically, if you're
interested in data or machine

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.030
learning in any form
or fashion, we've

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:44.730
likely got a competition
that will excite you.

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:47.430
And really, one of the most
exciting elements of Kaggle

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:50.640
is that as the home of data
science, professionals,

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.340
hobbyists, thought
leaders, and academics

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.210
will come to Kaggle to test
new theories, technologies,

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.620
and potential advancements in
the state of the art of machine

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:01.770
learning.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:04.200
Now we've been very
fortunate to witness

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.140
the birth, or the spread
and dissemination,

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:10.169
of many of the leading
data science techniques,

00:02:10.169 --> 00:02:11.900
from back when
random [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:17.340
decision trees took over, to
LightGBMs, XGBoost, and then

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.310
the introduction of
libraries, like Harris.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.160
So what better community to
introduce great technologies,

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:27.752
like TensorFlow and TPUs, than
through Kaggle competitions.

00:02:27.752 --> 00:02:30.210
And the Kaggle community is
filled with data scientists who

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:32.880
are eager to
experiment and quickly

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.090
uncover the benefits
and shortcomings

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:39.180
