products and methodologies.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:40.650
And a Kaggle
competition that can

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:43.590
provide a structured
and engaging format

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:44.820
to test them out.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.456
And specifically for
TPUs and TensorFlow,

00:02:47.456 --> 00:02:50.890
our competition platform allows
us to host really large data

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:55.350
sets, and to offer both time
and resource-constrained formats

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:58.070
that can help truly
capture the power

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:02.060
and understand the benefits
of these particular tools.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:04.940
So if you'd like to check out,
the latest TensorFlow edition,

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:07.220
we're calling it 2.x,
as they continue to be

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:09.020
updated on the Kaggle platform.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:12.690
Especially with GPU or
TPU, Kaggle makes it easy.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.360
You can see from the
list on this slide

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.790
that we've eliminated
a lot of the obstacles

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:21.050
to making TensorFlow 2.x build
your models fast, with next

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:22.950
to no specialized coding.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:26.720
No provisioning of the
right kind of VM, no setup.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:29.630
We already have data sets
that are ready to go.

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:31.970
Our cloud notebook
platform removes the burden

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:35.960
of needing to reconcile versions
or libraries or packages

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:40.670
across collaborators, so sharing
and iterating is a breeze.

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:43.120
And finally, we even
provide a weekly allotment

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:47.390
to TPU and GPU time at
no cost to our users

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:49.271
all over the world.

00:03:49.271 --> 00:03:51.770
And the integration benefits
afforded to TensorFlow

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:53.820
also apply to TPUs.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:56.180
They're directly accessible
from archival notebooks

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:57.770
that [INAUDIBLE]
additional setup.

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:59.228
We have generous
data sets that are

00:03:59.228 --> 00:04:01.490
accessible via Google
Cloud Storage that allow

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:03.590
for efficient data access.

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:08.250
30 hours of TPU use per week
are granted to each user.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:10.640
We've even hosted
five competitions just

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:14.840
in the last six months aimed
at these particular tools.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:16.709
Most recently, we
hosted a competition

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:20.279
that was TPU-centric with
the Google Team, Jigsaw, that

00:04:20.279 --> 00:04:22.580
was seeking to
evaluate and classify

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:25.112
toxic comments on Wikipedia.

00:04:25.112 --> 00:04:26.570
We also just launched
a competition

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:29.630
called Contradictory,
my dear Watson, which

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:31.910
seeks to use TPUs
and natural language

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:34.727
processing to evaluate grammar.

00:04:34.727 --> 00:04:36.310
But today we want
to look specifically

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:39.640
at our first two competitions
that introduced TPUs to over

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:42.250
five million data scientists.

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:45.400
First being TensorFlow
2.0 Question Answering,

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:48.760
the second being one on
flower classification.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:50.640
Now TensorFlow is
already very heavily used

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:51.780
in our competitions.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:54.510
It's currently being used in
all of our running competitions,

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:55.950
but in the past
few months, we've

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:58.620
run two TensorFlow
competitions that I mentioned,

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:02.610
this one on top being a question
answering NLP competition built

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:04.880
around the launch
TensorFlow 2.0,

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.200
