00:05:23.837 --> 00:05:25.670
And so next what I want
to do is, let's dive

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:26.653
in to some of the code.

00:05:26.653 --> 00:05:28.320
Let's look at some
of the code right now

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:31.800
we'll build a model on Kaggle
for our competition that

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:37.802
classify flowers on CPU,
GPU, and TPU with no changes.

00:05:37.802 --> 00:05:39.510
So Phil, I'm going to
hand it over to you

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:42.420
to walk through some of the
[INAUDIBLE] competitions.

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:44.240
PHIL CULLITON: Thanks, Addison.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:47.480
We start off simple by importing
TensorFlow 2 and the Kaggle

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:49.400
data sets library.

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:51.525
The rest of the code
here does a bit of magic.

00:05:51.525 --> 00:05:53.150
It figures out what
kind of accelerator

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:55.850
is attached to your VM, and
allows us to automatically

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:57.797
parallelize your model build.

00:05:57.797 --> 00:05:59.630
This will determine an
appropriate strategy,

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:03.890
whether you're using a V3
TPU, or a simple CPU VM.

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:06.800
Basically, this code asks for
a TPU, and if it gets one,

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:08.840
it connects to it
and sets it up.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:11.420
If it can't get a TPU,
it sets it up as well.

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:12.920
The rest of the
code in these slides

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:15.960
is entirely
accelerator agnostic.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.090
Normally, outside
of Kaggle and Colab,

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:20.100
you'd need to provision
this TPU for usage

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:21.560
and do some other setup.

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:23.680
But on our platform,
that's not necessary.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:25.560
Just select the TPU
from a dropdown and go.

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:30.440
Now we load up our
competition data set.

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:34.160
It's provided and sharded TF
record format for fast loading.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:36.170
We use multiple files
to provide enough data

00:06:36.170 --> 00:06:39.340
streams to maximize
the TPU's throughput.

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:41.770
First, we get the path
to those TF record files

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:44.770
and pass it to TensorFlow,
then set some parameters

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:46.690
for a training and
validation sets.

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:48.430
Shuffling our
training set, batching

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:50.362
both sets for
optimal performance,

00:06:50.362 --> 00:06:52.570
setting our training set to
repeat so it'll loop back

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:54.173
around with the [INAUDIBLE].

00:06:54.173 --> 00:06:56.090
We'll pass these into
our model and TensorFlow

00:06:56.090 --> 00:06:57.970
will access these
TF record files

00:06:57.970 --> 00:06:59.860
while the model is building.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:01.270
The end result of
this process is

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:02.837
that we have data
sets ready to run

00:07:02.837 --> 00:07:04.045
in our accelerator of choice.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:08.580
This code does
some heavy lifting.

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:12.120
Here, we load up VGG16 for
some transfer learning,

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:13.920
and then define
our model by adding

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:17.430
the global average pooling and
output layer on top of that.

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:20.280
Note the with
strategy.scope block.

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:22.470
That's basically saying,
hey, whichever type

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:24.850
of acceleration strategy
you're using here,

00:07:24.850 --> 00:07:27.180
this is the process
to parallelize.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.280
Now our model will be
compiled and trained

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:31.680
on the accelerator we've chosen.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:35.790
Note again that this same code
will run on CPU, GPU, or TPU.

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:38.280
As long as we identify
our distribution strategy,

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.020
TensorFlow 2
automatically handles

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:43.060
putting this model in the
right place and building.

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:45.670
After this, we compile our model
with our choice of optimizer

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:47.950
and loss, pass the training
and validation sets

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:50.062
from the previous slide
into it, and start

00:07:50.062 --> 00:07:51.145
the model-fitting process.

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:56.830
Making some flower predictions
is easy after this.

00:07:56.830 --> 00:07:59.260
We load up our test data set
using the same type of code

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:01.810
that we used to load the
training validation sets.

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:03.370
Once again, this
results in data that

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:05.620
makes the best use of our TPU.

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:08.570
Then we run model.predict,
transform our prediction

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:11.530
into the right form, and write
our predictions out in a format

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:12.790
that Kaggle can read.

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:14.680
With a few tweaks and
a few more epochs,

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:18.525
this model can actually
score pretty well.

00:08:18.525 --> 00:08:20.900
I'll finish the code section
with some of the fun flowers

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:22.777
that our model has
made predictions on.

00:08:22.777 --> 00:08:25.110
You can see that some are
right, they've got a little OK

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:26.937
above them, and some are wrong.

00:08:26.937 --> 00:08:28.520
If you're interested,
you're more than

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:30.980
welcome to drop by the Petals
to the Metal competition

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:32.780
and beat these results.

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:35.690
That won't be hard, but
it will probably be fun.

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:37.130
And now, Addison, back to you.

00:08:40.182 --> 00:08:41.390
ADDISON HOWARD: Thanks, Phil.

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:44.840
As Phil demonstrated in his code
review, using TPUs on Kaggle,

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:49.340
it's a super easy way
to get used to TPUs.

00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:51.530
In the flower competition--
flower classification

00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:53.330
competition that
Phil highlighted--

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:58.260
we had over 24,000 TPU-using
