can then quickly teach others.

00:09:36.852 --> 00:09:38.860
So let's highlight some
very specific outcomes

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:40.095
of this competition.

00:09:40.095 --> 00:09:41.470
In the Kaggle
notebook, users can

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:45.460
elect to use a CPU, GPU, or
a TPU, like Phil mentioned.

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:47.940
So we picked two of the
top-performing models using

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:52.850
each TPUs and GPUs that each to
use effectively the same code.

00:09:52.850 --> 00:09:57.780
The TPU model trained on
25,000 images in an hour and 15

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:01.140
minutes, and that same model
would run out of memory when

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:03.320
they try to flip it over GPUs.

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:06.150
The comparative GPU
model took five hours

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:08.950
to train those
same 25,000 images,

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:11.200
but they had to be
at a reduced size.

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:14.070
In the end, the accuracy
still did prove competitive

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:16.560
